          "input": "const patch = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Add File: /tmp/llame-issue-262-body.md\\n+## Problem\\n+\\n+A completed foreground run can produce a `Reply ready — <question>` toast even when the user is already viewing that chat. The toast then renders over the composer and intercepts pointer events on the Send button until it is dismissed.\\n+\\n+This is two coupled product defects, not merely a toast-placement preference:\\n+\\n+1. **Notification ownership race.** `ActiveRunsProvider` decides whether a terminal run belongs to the visible chat from a one-time `pathnameRef.current` snapshot. `ChatPage.onFinish` separately untracks the chat and performs the post-first-message route replacement. If terminal polling wins that race, a reply the user just watched is misclassified as a background completion and emits a toast.\\n+2. **Unsafe notification placement.** The global Sonner host uses its default bottom-right placement, which overlaps the chat composer's model/send controls. A legitimate background notification can therefore block primary chat interaction too.\\n+\\n+## Evidence\\n+\\n+Playwright captured the notification subtree intercepting the Send click:\\n+\\n+```\\n+attempting click action\\n+  - <div data-title=\\\"\\\">Reply ready — Tell me about the roadmap</div> from\\n+    <section aria-label=\\\"Notifications alt+T\\\"> subtree intercepts pointer events\\n+  - retrying click action\\n+```\\n+\\n+The historical mixed-symptom report is #163, now marked as a duplicate of this issue. Its retry-auth 404 and resume-state crash were separate defects fixed by #258; the notification race and interaction obstruction remain.\\n+\\n+The current `compaction-checkpoint.spec.ts` explicitly waits for the toast to disappear before sending the next message. That stabilizes the test; it does not fix the product behavior.\\n+\\n+## Impact\\n+\\n+- A user who watched a reply finish can receive a redundant \\\"Reply ready\\\" notification for that same reply.\\n+- While the toast is visible, the composer Send control can be unclickable.\\n+- The failure is bounded by toast dismissal, but it blocks the primary action at exactly the point a user is likely to continue the conversation.\\n+\\n+## Expected behavior\\n+\\n+- A run completed while its chat is visible in an active tab does not emit a background-completion toast, regardless of whether terminal polling or the stream's `onFinish` callback observes completion first.\\n+- Any legitimate notification displayed over a chat route must not overlap or intercept the composer at supported viewport sizes.\\n+\\n+## Acceptance criteria\\n+\\n+- Add a deterministic regression covering terminal polling winning the race against `onFinish`/route replacement; no arbitrary sleeps or forced clicks.\\n+- Verify that a foreground completion produces neither a completion toast nor an unread badge.\\n+- Verify that a legitimate background completion notification leaves the composer and Send button operable on desktop and mobile layouts.\\n+- Preserve interaction with notification actions; globally disabling pointer events is not an acceptable shortcut.\\n+\\n+## Implementation boundary\\n+\\n+The exact visual placement is a `DESIGN.md` decision, but accepting interaction obstruction is not. Likely solution boundaries are:\\n+\\n+- make notification eligibility depend on stable foreground ownership rather than an instantaneous pathname snapshot; and\\n+- place or offset the notification viewport so it cannot cover chat controls.\\n+\\n+Do not solve this by widening the e2e timeout, waiting longer in product code, or forcing the click.\\n*** End Patch\";\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(patch));\n",
